; A tribute to the original Breeze's # operator 
; (https://breeze-language.sourceforge.net/)

; The basic principle was that the language recognized 
; only numbers and lists of numbers.
; The # operator was used to turn numbers into code to be executed.

(word apply) (#) PUSH

; To push values onto the stack requires a prior meta-programming 
; op (used in disguise below): "word" # (i.e., "word" word apply)

; With this definition, the language can run on numbers, lists 
; (empty or containing numbers), and the sole special operator #, 
; as in Breeze!

; Let's try! Below this point only numbers and lists will be used 
; alongside the # operator.

; include the I/O module for printing
    (105 110 99 108 117 100 101 47 105 111 46 98 111) 
    (114 101 113 117 105 114 101) 
#

; Using the definition of # we execute the word ps as follows.
; Remember that a string is just a list of numbers.
; For example, the string "ps" is represented as the list (112 115)

    10                  ; just to see something on the stack
    (112 115) 
#                       ; this should show 0: 10

; drop the top of the default stack
    () (68 82 79 80) 
#

; Below, we define and use `succ` (adding 1 to the element on top 
; of the default stack) using this approach:

; define `succ`
    (
    1 
        (43) 
    #
    ) 
    (115 117 99 99) 
    (119 111 114 100) 
# 
    (80 85 83 72) 
#

; push 11, duplicate, and call `succ`
    11 
    0 () 
    (80 73 67 75) 
# 
    (115 117 99 99) 
#

; print the stack
    (112 115) 
#                       ; this should show 1: 11 and 0: 12
